In a frame rotating with angular velocity $bold(omega)$, the Coriolis force on a particle moving with velocity $bold(v)$ in that frame is
$
  bold(F_"cor") = -2 m bold(omega) times bold(v).
$
This is similar to the Lorentz force with $bold(B) -> bold(omega)$, so moving particles turn in circles.

On Earth, neglecting the small wobbling of the rotation axis gives no Euler force, and a falling body then obeys
$
  bold(dot.double(x)) = bold(g) - 2 bold(omega) times bold(dot(x)),
$
so integrating once,
$
  bold(dot(x)) = bold(g) t - 2 bold(omega) times (bold(x) - bold(x_0)).
$
